{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>Perhaps there shall lack five of the fifty righteous.</b> He should have said מֵחֲמִשִּׁים with a mem, or he should have said 'perhaps there shall be there forty-five.' And that which Rashi explained — that he requested by means of joining (צֵרוּף) — the essence is missing from the text, for צֵרוּף — who mentions its name? [That is, the word צֵרוּף does not appear in the verse at all.] And that which seems right to me to say regarding this is: because in all the requests the intention was that there should be ten for each city, and in the first request he wanted to save all five cities, for so the language implies — ״וְנָשָׂאתִי לְכָל הַמָּקוֹם בַּעֲבוּרָם״ (Genesis 18:26) — which was not said afterward. Now in the second request he likewise intended to save all of them, but he was responding to the word of HaShem, blessed be He, who had said ״אִם אֶמְצָא בִסְדֹם״ (Genesis 18:26) — which implies that they would all be found present in their homes specifically. Therefore he asked: perhaps five of those fifty have gone away, and there would be nine for each city — is that not sufficient, given that there are fifty there in existence, aside from those missing from some of them? For such is the law regarding any matter of sanctity requiring ten: if they began with ten and some departed — they may complete it. And there are those who permit even beginning with nine by means of joining a minor, as found in Tur Oraḥ Ḥayim siman 55. And the language is precisely measured in what it says — ״אוּלַי יַחְסְרוּן חֲמִשִּׁים הַצַּדִּיקִים״ — implying that there are fifty righteous in existence, only that five are lacking from them and have gone away, as stated. ״וַיֹּאמֶר לֹא אַשְׁחִית אִם אֶמְצָא שָׁם אַרְבָּעִים וַחֲמִשָּׁה״ (Genesis 18:28). He did not say that He would not destroy for the sake of the forty-five, because He wished to say that for the sake of the fifty He would not destroy, even though they are not all there — for only forty-five would be found there in their homes. And through this is precisely explained what is said ״בַּעֲבוּר הָאַרְבָּעִים״ (Genesis 18:29), ״בַּעֲבוּר הָעֶשְׂרִים״ (Genesis 18:30), ״בַּעֲבוּר הָעֲשָׂרָה״ (Genesis 18:32), whereas here He changed and said ״אִם אֶמְצָא שָׁם אַרְבָּעִים וַחֲמִשָּׁה״. Rather, He wished to say: if these should be there, even though the five are not there — and in any case it is required that they had been there and then departed." "And regarding what is said by the number thirty — ״אִם אֶמְצָא שָׁם שְׁלֹשִׁים״ — which implies that all of them must be there, we may resolve this by noting that to save three cities with the number thirty is the weakest of all, for this reason it is said regarding it ״אַל יִחַר לַאדֹנָי וַאֲדַבֵּרָה״, which was not said regarding any of the others — implying that there is no basis for this request, because all the numbers he mentioned possess an aspect of merit and an aspect of virtue that would protect them. For we find fifty gates of understanding in the world, and we find that the Torah was given in forty days, and we find twenty righteous men from Adam the first man until Abraham — that is, the entire chain of descent mentioned at the end of Genesis and at the end of the portion of Noah — and we find ten utterances by which the world was created. If so, there is certainly an aspect of holiness in these numbers; therefore it is just that they should protect a corresponding number of righteous. But in the number thirty Abraham found in his reasoning no special aspect of holiness; therefore he said of it ״אַל יִחַר לַאדֹנָי״, and HaShem agreed with him, in His saying ״אִם אֶמְצָא שָׁם״ — all of them must be there, for they have nothing on which to rely, as do all the other numbers which have an aspect of holiness from somewhere else. And the complete holy number that they possess from elsewhere serves to protect corresponding numbers, even when not all are in their homes." "And through this is explained what is said regarding all of them — ״לֹא אַשְׁחִית״ — whereas by the number thirty He said ״לֹא אֶעֱשֶׂה״. For ״לֹא אַשְׁחִית״ is translated [in the Targum] לָא אֲחַבֵּל, and it means: not even the slightest injury and not even the slightest destruction — not even through afflictions will I do, for the holiness of that number. But ״לֹא אֶעֱשֶׂה״ is translated לָא אֶעְבֵיד גְּמֵירָא, and it means: I will not destroy them completely — but in any case I will inflict upon them some injury and destruction through afflictions, as it is written: ״עָשׂוּ כָּלָה וְאִם לֹא אֵדָעָה״ (Jeremiah 5:1). That is because the number thirty is not so fortified and esteemed as to protect even against afflictions." "And regarding what is said by the number forty — ״לֹא אֶעֱשֶׂה״ — even though it is an esteemed number from the aspect of the Torah given in forty days, in any case, since the Torah had not yet been given and was yet to be given, therefore this number does not protect against afflictions as the other numbers do. Therefore it is like the intermediate among the rest of the numbers whose character had already emerged in the world for holiness and splendor. And furthermore, we find its opposite as well: ״אַרְבָּעִים יַכֶּנּוּ״ (Deuteronomy 25:3), and that is a prosecutor and not a defender; therefore this number is intermediate, and it is easy to understand. And we find among the commentators many opinions in resolving these difficulties; we have also found that they gave several allusions by way of derash for these numbers. Therefore my heart is full to go in their footsteps, to make in it allusions close to reason, in order to resolve through them all the variations we found in these requests — for it is not an empty thing. And even if indeed I have erred in what is seen, I am no worse than what the Mahar[i]a, of blessed memory, wrote, who likewise made remote allusions and sealed his words, and this is his language: ״And it is possible that there may be other reasons in this, and even these are nothing but a way of asmakhta and derash, for the material of derash will not tolerate more than this״ — end of his words."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "The Aramaic phrase מַאן דְּכַר שְׁמֵיהּ (literally 'who mentions its name') is a talmudic idiom meaning the thing is never explicitly named; rendered literally to preserve the idiom."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:29:12.177130734Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}